In "Re: [PATCH] claude/skills/spec-report: Report status of the spec"
[1] you asked for a clear example of the naming problem. Here it is.

The rule only replaces the prefix and keeps the extension. So these
are the specs the examples imply and what the rule makes of them:

    spec.org -> report.org
    SPEC.md -> REPORT.md
    spec--<name>.org -> report--<name>.org
    SPEC--<name>.md -> REPORT--<name>.md

The example list names =REPORT--<name>.org=, but no spec the other
examples suggest produces it. Only an upper-case org spec,
SPEC--<name>.org, would. A reader who takes the example list at face
value expects SPEC--<name>.md to give REPORT--<name>.org, and the rule
says REPORT--<name>.md.

The skill follows the rule, so it works either way. To make the
example agree with the rule, the example could read
=REPORT--<name>.md=. If you did mean an upper-case org spec, the
example is right as it is and nothing needs to change.

This is your spec, carried as a prerequisite in the series "[PATCH v3
0/4] Add the spec-report skill and its report" [2]. You took patches
2-4 squashed into b/dev as "claude/skills/spec-report: New skill",
and patch 1 is on trunk, so the series needs nothing more from me.
